WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:09.630
This is the debrief for Ship labtools 0.1.0, where two functions become a package that passes every package check.

2
00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:13.970
First, the public interface and its documentation come from roxygen comments.

3
00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:16.950
Second, every example runs exactly as written.

4
00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:21.770
Third, every function from another package is declared, so the check finds nothing undefined.

5
00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:27.600
Each exported function documents every parameter and its return value.

6
00:00:27.790 --> 00:00:31.540
The @examples section uses small inline data, so it runs on any machine.

7
00:00:31.810 --> 00:00:34.300
The @export tag puts clean_site() in the NAMESPACE.

8
00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:36.200
Helpers without it stay internal.

9
00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:42.460
The body is the rule from the first challenge: trim, upper-case, and insert the hyphen, with NA left as NA.

10
00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:48.740
summarise_arm() follows the same pattern: parameters, return value, and an example.

11
00:00:48.970 --> 00:00:51.200
The @importFrom lines are what most attempts miss.

12
00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:55.940
group_by() and summarise() come from dplyr, and sd() comes from stats, not base R.

13
00:00:56.050 --> 00:01:01.360
The body returns one row per arm, and passes na_rm straight through to mean() and sd().

14
00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:05.560
Three mistakes account for most failed attempts.

15
00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:10.920
Calling group_by() or sd() without importing them leaves undefined globals, and the globals check fails.

16
00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:15.900
An example that reads a file the reader doesn't have will not run, and the examples check fails.

17
00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:20.840
And a missing @param or @return tag leaves the help page incomplete, which fails the docs check.

18
00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:24.940
This solution passes all seven checks.

19
00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:30.940
The validator self-test runs this exact code against every one of them, so what you see here is what the boss grades.

20
00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:32.920
Your own solution can look different.

21
00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:35.300
What matters is that it passes the same checks.
